content:
REFERENCE. OSFI Guideline B-20, Residential Mortgage Underwriting Practices and Procedures, sets the Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions' expectations for federally regulated financial institutions in Canada regarding residential mortgage underwriting, including governance, borrower due diligence, loan-to-value policy and portfolio risk management.

STRAIGHT RENEWAL POSITION. Where a mortgage is renewed with the same borrower, no increase in principal, and no extension of amortization beyond the original contractual amortization remaining, the bank treats the transaction as a straight renewal. Full re-underwriting, income re-verification and debt-service re-testing are not required for a straight renewal, consistent with OSFI's position that the minimum qualifying rate stress test does not apply to a straight renewal with the existing lender.

WHEN B-20 EXPECTATIONS APPLY IN FULL. If any of the following conditions is present, the transaction is a new underwriting decision and B-20 expectations for income verification, total debt service and gross debt service testing, and property valuation apply in full:
- the borrower changes, including addition or removal of a covenantor;
- the principal increases for any reason, including capitalisation of fees or arrears;
- the amortization is extended beyond the original contractual amortization remaining;
- additional funds are advanced.

PORTFOLIO RISK MANAGEMENT. B-20 expects institutions to manage residential mortgage portfolio risk in aggregate, not only at the file level. Retention activity that concentrates high loan-to-value or high payment-shock exposure at discounted pricing must be monitored against internal portfolio limits. Monthly reporting of retained volume must be split by loan-to-value band and by payment-shock band; retention rate alone is an insufficient control measure.

LTV AND INSURANCE. Loan-to-value above 80 percent requires mortgage default insurance. Renewal pricing must not be used to retain a file that would otherwise breach loan-to-value policy.

DOCUMENTATION. Where the straight-renewal treatment is relied upon, the file must evidence that all four conditions above were tested and satisfied. Loose interpretation of straight renewal is an internal audit finding.

RETENTION IS NOT RISK-NEUTRAL. Retaining a file at a discounted rate retains the underlying credit exposure. A retention campaign that succeeds disproportionately with high loan-to-value and high payment-shock borrowers concentrates exactly the exposure that OSFI Guideline B-20 portfolio limits exist to control.

MONITORING REQUIREMENTS. The monthly renewal pack must report:
- retained volume and balance split by loan-to-value band (below 65, 65 to 80, above 80);
- retained volume and balance split by payment-shock band (below 25 percent, 25 to 60 percent, above 60 percent);
- uninsured share of retained balance;
- average discount granted, by branch and by segment, against the 20 basis point campaign threshold in RP-001;
- pricing exception count and value, by branch, against RP-002;
- fast-path amortization approvals and the resulting portfolio amortization profile shift, per RP-006.

MARGIN EROSION. Each basis point of discount applied across the maturing book is a permanent reduction in net interest income for the term. Discount decisions must be assessed against relationship value over the term, not against the mortgage margin alone; conversely, relationship value may not be asserted without evidence of the deposits, investments or other products relied upon.

CONDUCT RISK. Advisor incentives weighted toward retention volume, combined with widened discretionary authority, create pressure to discount without evidence. The mandatory rationale field, the tiered evidence standard and the quarterly exception report are the compensating controls; degradation in any one of them is an escalation trigger.

OPERATIONAL RISK. Reliance on manually distributed pricing grids has produced at least two instances of advisors quoting superseded rates. Pricing must be published from a single authoritative source with a visible effective-from date and version history. Consistent with OSFI Guideline E-21 expectations for operational risk management and resilience, a control that depends on manual redistribution is not an adequate control.

CONCENTRATION BY CHANNEL. Broker-originated renewals behave materially differently from branch-originated renewals - lower contactability, higher propensity to move at maturity. They require a named owner and a distinct retention playbook. An unowned cohort is an unmanaged exposure.
